-- Q1587: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 500.06, sufficiency is the measure of the quantity of audit evidence. The quantity needed is affected by the auditor''s assessment of the risk of material misstatement and the quality of evidence obtained. Higher risk requires more evidence; higher-quality evidence may reduce the quantity needed.
Wrong (B): Relevance and reliability describe appropriateness under AU-C 500.07, which measures the quality of evidence — a distinct concept from sufficiency.
Wrong (C): Timeliness may affect evidence quality but is not the definition of sufficiency, which specifically addresses quantity.
Wrong (D): Cost-effectiveness is a practical consideration in audit planning but does not define the concept of sufficiency under auditing standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1587;

-- Q1588: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 500.07, appropriateness is the measure of the quality of audit evidence, encompassing both relevance (whether the evidence relates to the assertion being tested) and reliability (the trustworthiness of the evidence given its source and nature).
Wrong (A): The number of items tested relates to sufficiency under AU-C 500.06, which measures quantity — not the quality dimension of evidence.
Wrong (B): The total cost of the engagement is an administrative consideration that has no bearing on whether evidence is relevant or reliable.
Wrong (C): The client''s willingness to provide documentation may create a scope limitation but does not define what makes evidence appropriate.$EXPL$
